  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Saturday...\n\nSlowly-evolving upper low over the PacNW and anomalously high\natmospheric moisture in the SW flow aloft (aided by remnants of\nHurricane Rachel) will continue to bring significant precipitation\nto especially the west half of the cwa today through midday\nMonday. All winter highlights look good and will make no changes\nat this time. Boundary layer cooling in the west has happened \nfaster since Saturday evening than previously thought, but it is \nuncertain if this will translate to forecast differences moving \nforward. \n\nConfidence is high that everywhere east of Musselshell to Sheridan\nCounties will see only rain with this system. There is simply not\nenough cooler or drier air to advect through eastern MT. Dewpts in\nnortheast MT are in the lower 40s. Coolest Canadian air over the \npast 12hrs has surged through central MT, giving further credence \nto what we are seeing in the models. Additionally, greatest precip\nwith this system (1-1.5\" and locally up to 2\") will be from \nBillings westward.\n\nGiven the fast transition to snow along the western foothills it \nis possible that snowfall amounts in this area end up higher than \ncurrently forecast, but this is a tough call per the warm ground \nand marginal air temps. Have bumped up snow accums a bit and will\ncontinue to monitor. Locations east of the immediate foothills \nwill see only rain today, but their opportunity for wet snow comes\ntonight into Monday morning, coinciding with an impressive \nmoisture surge from the south and further cooling of the boundary \nlayer. The Bull Mountains are a solid candidate for several inches\nof snow given the elevation, and similarly the higher hills in \nGolden Valley and northern Stillwater (these zones have Winter \nWeather Advisories starting Sunday evening). A trickier forecast \nexists from Billings to Joliet and Red Lodge. Luther & Red Lodge \nshould see a little accumulation when all is said and done. Models\nshow a strong backing of mid level winds to easterly late Sunday \nas Rachel's wave approaches, which bodes well for enhanced ascent \nand further dynamic cooling into early Monday. There is a fair \nchance (40%) for measurable snow over the hills surrounding \nBillings. This will be something to watch closely today.\n\nThe wave exits Monday afternoon and precip should be tapered off \nby sunset. Surface high over eastern MT supports clearing and\nstrong potential for freezing temps & areas of fog Monday night\ninto early Tuesday. For much of the area this will be the first\nfrost/freeze of the fall. Tuesday is looking dry but proximity to\nthe cut off upper low over the great basin could bring a few\nshowers over the far south. Temps in the 30s & 40s today & Monday\nshould rise to the upper 40s to mid 50s Tuesday.\n\nRain showers are possible over southeast MT Wednesday as the \naforementioned upper low kicks thru WY/SD/NE...though there is \nuncertainty in its exact track and timing. Into Thursday there is \na Canadian clipper that drops into the northern plains. Today's \nmodels have trended further east and drier w/ this wave, but even \nthen there is a likelihood of breezy NW winds and moderately \ncooler temps. MSLP clusters show high uncertainty but if a \nreasonably strong/dry surface high clips eastern MT Thursday night\nit could provide a cold night with a hard freeze in the east. \nLook for high temps in the 50s to near 60F Wednesday & Thursday. \n\nThere is growing confidence for a building ridge to bring warming\nand drying to end the week. Friday should begin the warming,\nthough that depends on the strength of Thursday's clipper. Look\nfor highs back near 70F Saturday. JKL",
